Streams:

Stream in Java was introduced in Java 8 to simplify the processing of collections and data

        numbers.stream()
               .filter(num -> num > 20)
               .map(num -> num * 2)
               .collect(Collectors.toList());

Operation	Purpose
stream()	Creates a stream from the collection
filter()	Selects elements based on a condition
map()	    Transforms each element
collect()	Collects the result into a list


Intermediate vs Terminal Operations

Intermediate operations
- Return another Stream.
- Can be chained together.
- Examples: filter(), map(), sorted(), distinct().

Terminal operations
- End the stream pipeline.
- Produce a result or perform an action.
- Examples: collect(), forEach(), count(), reduce().


Two types of intermediate operations: Stateless and Stateful

Stateless	                                         Stateful
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Processes elements independently	         Considers multiple elements
Doesn't need previous elements	             May need to remember elements
Usually doesn't need to buffer the stream	 May need buffering
filter(), map()	                             sorted(), distinct()

Lazy evaluation means Java does not execute intermediate Stream operations immediately. It waits until a terminal operation is called

reduce() is used to combine all elements in a Stream into a single result.
Where do we use reduce()?
Common use cases:
- Sum of numbers.
- Product of numbers.
- Finding maximum or minimum.
- Combining values into one result.